**Read:** [Chemical Reactor Analysis and Design Fundamentals — Rawlings and Ekerdt](../sources/Chemical-Reactor-Analysis-Rawlings-Ekerdt.lingua#r_7YQAV27Y3J0B537Z6K5HDN8H0A), the entire heterogeneous-reaction chapter and relevant parameter/transport problems; [Chemical and Biological Reaction Engineering — MIT 10.37](../sources/Reaction-Engineering-MIT-10-37.lingua#r_67AEXD9K1DJ46SV942PAZHZ4BZ), the full heterogeneous and diffusion/heat-transfer programme; [Production of Large Volume Organic Chemicals, BREF LVOC](../sources/Large-Volume-Organic-Chemicals-BREF.lingua#r_78ET6A4D7AX8HPQTYAMZMAG16B), a complete relevant multiphase catalytic-process case.

Gas-liquid, liquid-liquid, gas-solid and gas-liquid-solid reactions combine chemical kinetics with contacting and phase behavior. Interfacial area, wetting, dissolution, adsorption, pore diffusion and local heat transfer influence the state at the reaction site. An overall fitted rate can hide several mechanisms.

For a catalyst particle, compare the rate predicted from a uniform internal composition with a model that resolves concentration and temperature gradients. An effectiveness factor is defined relative to that chosen reference state; it is not a universal efficiency. A change in pressure, particle size, pore state or heat removal can alter both the intrinsic and transport contributions.

For fluid-fluid reactions, distinguish transfer to the interface, dissolution, reaction in a film/bulk and product removal. A rapid reaction can enhance absorption while increasing local gradients or competing chemistry. Scale changes also affect distribution of phases and the residence of solids or droplets.

**Work:** reconstruct a full multiphase industrial reaction with primary kinetic, phase and transport evidence. Derive limiting cases, test parameter sensitivity and compare two plausible contacting arrangements at equal useful output. Explain which independent observations would distinguish a chemical-rate limitation from changed area, wetting, diffusion or temperature.

